# Changelog
## [0.4.0] - Unreleased
### Breaking changes
 - `L2tpAttribute::IfName` holds the new `L2tpIfName` instead of a `String`.
   No public API uses `String` for interface names any more.
 - `L2tpAttribute::if_name` takes `impl AsRef<[u8]>` instead of
   `impl Into<String>`. Callers passing a `char`, `Cow<str>`, `Box<str>` or
   `x.into()` can use `L2tpIfName::from_string` or `from_str_ref` instead.
 - Parsing `L2TP_ATTR_IFNAME` reads the payload up to the first NUL, as the
   kernel does, and ignores the rest. 0.3.1 rejected payloads with bytes
   after an embedded NUL.

### New features
 - `L2tpIfName`, a Linux interface name stored as the kernel's
   `char[IFNAMSIZ]` (`[u8; 16]`). Names need not be valid UTF-8, so
   interfaces with such names can be represented and parsed.
 - `L2tpIfName` is always valid: constructors apply the kernel's checks
   (1 to 15 bytes, and the `dev_valid_name()` rules), so emitting an
   interface name never panics.
 - Conversions: `L2tpIfName::new(impl AsRef<[u8]>)`,
   `from_str_ref(impl AsRef<str>)`, `from_string(impl Into<String>)`,
   `FromStr`, and `TryFrom` for `&str`, `String`, `&String`, `&[u8]`,
   `Vec<u8>` and `[u8; 16]`.
 - Accessors: `as_bytes()`, `as_array()`, `to_str()`, `to_string_lossy()`,
   `Display` (lossy), `AsRef<[u8]>` and `From<L2tpIfName> for [u8; 16]`.

### Bug fixes
 - N/A

## [0.3.1] - 2026-10-22
### Behaviour changes
 - Emitting an invalid attribute now panics instead of producing a message
   the kernel would reject. This covers invalid interface names (see below),
   cookies that are not 0, 4 or 8 bytes long, attributes
   too long for the 16-bit netlink length field, and known attribute types
   passed through `Other` with an invalid payload. Use the checked
   constructors `L2tpAttribute::if_name`, `cookie` and `peer_cookie` for
   user input.
 - `L2tpMessage::emit` checks every attribute and the output buffer size
   before writing anything, so a panic leaves the buffer untouched.

 - Interface names follow the kernel's `dev_valid_name()` rules in
   `L2tpAttribute::if_name`, emission and parsing: 1 to 15 bytes, not `.` or
   `..`, and no NUL, `/`, `:` or whitespace bytes. Whitespace includes byte
   0xa0, as the kernel's `isspace()` does, which rules out characters such
   as `à`. Empty names were accepted in 0.3.0 and are now rejected.

### Bug fixes
 - NUL padding after an interface name is accepted when parsing.
 - Parsing rejects nonempty payloads on flag and padding attributes.
 - Parsing an unchecked attribute buffer with a malformed header returns an
   error instead of panicking.
 - Attribute lengths are checked before the 16-bit length field could
   silently truncate them.
 - Errors for invalid raw attributes name the offending attribute type.
 - Each attribute is validated once per emission instead of repeatedly.

### Documentation
 - Document the validation rules and the remaining panicking APIs in the
   README.

## [0.3.0] - 2026-10-21
First release from https://github.com/rust-netlink/netlink-packet-l2tp, a
rewrite of the crate. Changes below are relative to the published 0.2.0.

### Breaking changes
 - api break: fix: encode L2TP_ATTR_DATA_SEQ as u8. (870c13d)
 - All public constants are removed (`L2TP_GENL_NAME`, `L2TP_GENL_VERSION`,
   `L2TP_GENL_MCGROUP`, `L2TP_CMD_*`, `L2TP_ATTR_*`, `L2TP_PWTYPE_*`,
   `L2TP_L2SPECTYPE_*`, `L2TP_ENCAPTYPE_*`, `L2TP_COOKIE_MAX_LEN` and
   `L2TP_IFNAME_MAX_LEN`). Use the enums and their `From` conversions
   instead.
 - `L2tpStatsAttr` is renamed to `L2tpStatsAttribute`, and its `StatsPad`
   variant to `Pad`.
 - `L2tpL2SpecType` is removed; `L2tpAttribute::L2SpecType` holds a `u8`.
 - `L2tpAttribute::UdpCsum`, `RecvSeq`, `SendSeq`, `LnsMode` and
   `UsingIpsec` hold a `u8` instead of a `bool`, so values other than 0 and
   1 survive a parse/emit round trip.
 - `L2tpAttribute::Fd` holds an `i32` instead of `RawFd`.
 - The per-command constructors `L2tpMessage::tunnel_create`,
   `session_get` and so on are replaced by `L2tpMessage::new(cmd)`.
 - Edition 2024 with a minimum supported Rust version of 1.89.
 - Depends on `netlink-packet-core` 0.9 and `netlink-packet-generic` 0.5
   instead of 0.8 and 0.4; the `libc` and `log` dependencies are dropped.

### New features
 - `L2tpMessage::new(cmd)` creates a message without attributes.

### Bug fixes
 - `L2TP_ATTR_STATS` is emitted without `NLA_F_NESTED`, matching the
   kernel, which builds this attribute with `nla_nest_start_noflag()`.
 - `L2TP_ATTR_FD` is encoded as a 32-bit two's complement integer: emitting
   a negative value no longer panics, and parsing a value above `i32::MAX`
   no longer fails.
 - docs: clarify UDP checksum and FD attribute semantics. (29ef892)
 - validate L2TP interface name length. (2a204d3)
 - validate L2TP cookie lengths. (ba969f5)
 - Enable GitHub CI. (2d876f5)

### Documentation
 - README and crate docs include a usage example and point to the `nl-l2tp`
   crate for talking to the kernel.

## [0.2.0] - 2026-03-28
Published from https://github.com/menhera-org/netlink-packet-l2tp; these
changes are not part of this repository's history.

### Breaking changes
 - `L2tpAttribute::DataSeq` holds a `u16` instead of a `u8` and is encoded
   as a 16-bit value. (Reverted in 0.3.0: the kernel policy is `NLA_U8`.)
 - `L2tpAttribute::Fd` holds a `RawFd` instead of a `u32`. Emitting a
   negative descriptor panics, and parsing a value above `i32::MAX` returns
   an error.

### New features
 - N/A

### Bug fixes
 - Cookies must be 0, 4 or 8 bytes long, both in `L2tpAttribute::cookie` and
   `peer_cookie` and when parsing. Previously any length up to 8 bytes was
   accepted.
 - Parsing `L2TP_ATTR_RECV_TIMEOUT` only accepts the 8-byte `u64` payload
   the kernel sends. A 4-byte payload is now an error instead of being
   widened.

## [0.1.0] - 2026-03-28
Initial release, published from
https://github.com/menhera-org/netlink-packet-l2tp; not part of this
repository's history.

### New features
 - `L2tpMessage`, the payload of the `l2tp` generic netlink family
   (version 1), implementing `GenlFamily`, `Emitable` and
   `ParseableParametrized<[u8], GenlHeader>`, with one constructor per
   command (`tunnel_create`, `session_get`, ...).
 - `L2tpCmd` covering every `L2TP_CMD_*` command, with `Other(u8)` for
   unknown values.
 - `L2tpAttribute` covering every `L2TP_ATTR_*` attribute, with
   `Other(DefaultNla)` for unknown types. Boolean attributes such as
   `UdpCsum`, `RecvSeq` and `LnsMode` use `bool`.
 - `L2tpStatsAttr` for the nested `L2TP_ATTR_STATS` counters.
 - `L2tpPwType`, `L2tpEncapType` and `L2tpL2SpecType` enums, each with an
   `Other` variant for unknown values.
 - Checked constructors `L2tpAttribute::if_name`, `cookie` and
   `peer_cookie`, rejecting interface names over 15 bytes and cookies over
   8 bytes. Parsing applies the same limits.
 - Public constants for the family name, version, multicast group, and all
   `L2TP_CMD_*`, `L2TP_ATTR_*`, `L2TP_PWTYPE_*`, `L2TP_L2SPECTYPE_*` and
   `L2TP_ENCAPTYPE_*` values.
 - `L2TP_ATTR_RECV_TIMEOUT` parsing accepts both 4-byte and 8-byte
   payloads.
